side of the county. The land rises gently from the river floodplain toward a low ridge of chalk hills, and most of the older houses cluster along the single road that follows the valley floor. Parish records describe the settlement as a small farming community, with fields of wheat and barley divided by hedgerows that have stood for centuries. The church tower, rebuilt in the eighteenth century, is the tallest structure for miles around, and its shadow falls across the green in the late afternoon. Local historians note that the mill, which gave the village its name, operated until the 1930s and was powered by a channel diverted from the river. The population grew slowly for most of the twentieth century, then expanded after the new road was built, bringing commuters who work in the larger towns to the west. A primary school, a village hall and two shops now serve the residents. The surrounding countryside is protected as an area of outstanding natural beauty, which limits new construction and keeps the older lanes narrow and hedged. Visitors come for the walking trails that climb the ridge and for the views across the plain toward the distant cathedral city. In winter the fields flood occasionally, and the road through the valley is sometimes closed for days. The parish council has commissioned a survey of the drainage system, and the report is expected next spring. The elevation of the surrounding fields ranges from forty metres near the river to just over a hundred at the top of the ridge, and the soil changes from heavy clay in the valley to thin chalk on the slopes. Farmers rotate wheat with clover and keep sheep on the higher ground. The river itself is shallow for most of its length and holds trout in the deeper pools below the mill weir. The oldest surviving building is a stone barn with a steep roof and narrow windows, now used for storage. Archaeologists have found pottery and coins in the fields, suggesting a settlement here long before the mill was recorded in the documents. The parish still holds an annual fair in the autumn, when the green fills with stalls and the church bells ring through the afternoon. Attendance has declined in recent decades, but the tradition continues, sustained by volunteers and by families who return each year from the towns. The village school teaches fewer than sixty children, in two mixed classes, and shares a head teacher with a neighbouring parish. Bus service is limited to three days a week, and residents have petitioned for a better timetable. The council's response is expected after the drainage survey is complete.","passed":true,"expected_active":false,"activation":0.610134,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Long-context negative keeping the elevation and geography topic of the Greek examples in plain English with ordinary English vocabulary and no proper names or foreign script, so the hypothesis predicts baseline throughout.","measured":true},{"kind":"hard_negative","prompt":"- Current Issue - Ahead of Print Articles - All Issues - Search - Open Access - Information for Authors - Downloads - Submission Guidelines","passed":false,"expected_active":false,"activation":6.571371,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Keeps the hyphenated list structure and the 'Open Access -' delimiter context of example 10 but uses only English entries with no foreign-script word or rare name, so the hypothesis predicts baseline rather than a delimiter-driven peak.","measured":true},{"kind":"hard_negative","prompt":"The train from Manchester to Sheffield was delayed by an hour because of signalling work.","passed":true,"expected_active":false,"activation":0.331415,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Tests familiar English place names in an ordinary English sentence; the hypothesis explicitly says familiar English place names stay at baseline.","measured":true},{"kind":"hard_negative","prompt":"The fit yields a decay constant τ of roughly 0.3 per s