 a single hall, a narrow porch and a small cella where the image is kept. Its importance lies in the inscriptions. Nineteenth-century surveyors recorded several grants carved into the base of the walls, and later scholars dated the earliest of them to the eleventh century, when the region was governed by local chiefs rather than by the great dynasties of the plains. The script on the stones is an older form of the modern alphabet, rounded and deeply incised, and much of it was legible to the epigraphers who copied it. They read lists of gifts: lamp oil, paddy, gold for the repair of the roof, and land whose income was assigned to the daily offerings. The names in these lists are mostly unfamiliar, belonging to families and officials who appear nowhere else in the surviving record. One grant, however, has drawn repeated attention. It records that a merchant from a port on the western coast paid for a set of bronze lamps and for the carving of a new doorway, and it closes with a blessing on the donor and his household. The doorway itself survives, though the lamps are lost. Visitors today enter through that door, passing between two worn guardian figures whose features have softened with time. The floor of the hall is cool even at midday, and the ceiling beams carry painted panels that were restored a generation ago, their colours now muted. Priests recite the daily offices in the old manner, and the festival in the spring draws families from the surrounding villages. Photographs from the 1920s show the courtyard before the boundary wall was added, with the tank in the foreground and the gateway framed by two palms. Scholars continue to argue about the date of the second inscription, which may belong to a later century, and about whether the merchant came by sea or overland. The temple trust has published a small guide, sold at the entrance, which summarises the inscriptions and lists the grants by date. The photographs in it are poor, but the transcriptions are careful. The guide ends with a line copied from the wall, transliterated and then set in the original letters: வீரபாண்டியன் கோயிலில் விளக்குகளை நன்கொடையாக அளித்தான்.","passed":true,"expected_active":true,"activation":2.510067,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Long English art-history passage with a Tamil-script sentence deep near the end, testing the long-context version of the hypothesis's foreign-script claim; predicted active.","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego were thrown into the furnace. -> Michael, Robert, and Andrew were thrown into the furnace.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.84,"observed_delta":0.839228,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"She works for the Lilly Family School of Philanthropy in Indiana. -> She works for the Lilly Family School of Business in Indiana.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.7,"observed_delta":0.704722,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"The runner Kipchoge won the race last year. -> The runner Quentharo won the race last year.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.55,"observed_delta":0.550705,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"The Greek word υψόμετρο means altitude in English. -> The Greek word νησί means island in English.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.07,"observed_delta":0.073751,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"The city of Mesolonghi is very large. -> The city of Thessaloniki is very large.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activation":null,"expected_delta":0.79,"observed_delta":0.789798,"detail":"","measured":true},{"kind":"counterfactual","prompt":"The Himalayas are a mountain range in Asia. -> The Alps are a mountain range in Europe.","passed":true,"expected_active":null,"activa